        ":question/id": "bdb5ab47-cea8-5da9-89b4-45a9046d7661",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-35734",
        ":question/problem": "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $B$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns."
