        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-73639",
        ":question/problem": "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th and $6$th columns."
